"Internal Error ()\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.fusejs.io/api/options.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.fusejs.io/api/options.html: (404) Not Found\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHTML Standard (https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#implicit-submission\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2882\nL0: # HTML\nL1: \nL2: Living Standard — Last Updated 9 October 2026\nL3: \nL4: cite0†← 4.10.6 The button element — cite1†Table of Contents — cite2†4.11 Interactive elements → L5:   1.     1.       1. cite3†4.10.18 Form control infrastructure L6:         1. cite4†4.10.18.1 A form control's value L7:         2. cite5†4.10.18.2 Mutability L8:         3. cite6†4.10.18.3 Association of controls and forms L9:       2. cite7†4.10.19 Attributes common to form controls L10:         1. cite8†4.10.19.1 Naming form controls: the `name` attribute L11:         2. cite9†4.10.19.2 Submitting element directionality: the `dirname` attribute L12:         3. cite10†4.10.19.3 Limiting user input length: the `maxlength` attribute L13:         4. cite11†4.10.19.4 Setting minimum input length requirements: the `minlength` attribute L14:         5. cite12†4.10.19.5 Enabling and disabling form controls: the `disabled` attribute L15:         6. cite13†4.10.19.6 Form submission attributes L16:         7. cite14†4.10.19.7 Autofill L17:           1. cite15†4.10.19.7.1 Autofilling form controls: the `autocomplete` attribute L18:           2. cite16†4.10.19.7.2 Processing model L19:       3. cite17†4.10.20 APIs for the text control selections L20:       4. cite18†4.10.21 Constraints L21:         1. cite19†4.10.21.1 Definitions L22:         2. cite20†4.10.21.2 Constraint validation L23:         3. cite21†4.10.21.3 The constraint validation API L24:         4. cite22†4.10.21.4 Security L25:       5. cite23†4.10.22 Form submission L26:         1. cite24†4.10.22.1 Introduction L27:         2. cite25†4.10.22.2 Implicit submission L28:         3. cite26†4.10.22.3 Form submission algorithm L29:         4. cite27†4.10.22.4 Constructing the entry list L30:         5. cite28†4.10.22.5 Selecting a form submission encoding L31:         6. cite29†4.10.22.6 Converting an entry list to a list of name-value pairs L32:         7. cite30†4.10.22.7 URL-encoded form data L33:         8. cite31†4.10.22.8 Multipart form data L34:         9. cite32†4.10.22.9 Plain text form data L35:         10. cite33†4.10.22.10 The `SubmitEvent` interface L36:         11. cite34†4.10.22.11 The `FormDataEvent` interface L37:       6. cite35†4.10.23 Resetting a form L38: #### 4.10.18 Form control infrastructure\nL39: ##### 4.10.18.1 A form control's value\nL40: \nL41: Most form controls have a value and a checkedness. (The latter is only used by `cite36†input ` elements.) These are used to describe how the user interacts with the control.\nL42: \nL43: A control's cite37†value is its internal state. As such, it might not match the user's current input.\nL44: For instance, if a user enters the word \"`three`\" into cite38†a numeric field that expects digits, the user's input would be the string \"three\" but the control's cite37†value would remain unchanged. Or, if a user enters the email address \"`  awesome@example.com`\" (with leading whitespace) into cite39†an email field , the user's input would be the string \"  awesome@example.com\" but the browser's UI for email fields might translate that into a cite37†value of \"`awesome@example.com`\" (without the leading whitespace).\nL45: `cite36†input ` and `cite40†textarea ` elements have a dirty value flag. This is used to track the interaction between the cite37†value and default value. If it is false, cite37†value mirrors the default value. If it is true, the default value is ignored.\nL46: \nL47: Some form controls also have an optional value. This largely mirrors the cite37†value but doesn't normalize to an empty string. This ought to be used sparingly, you generally want cite37†value .\nL48: `cite36†input `, `cite40†textarea `, and `cite41†select ` elements have a user validity boolean. It is initially set to false.\nL49: \nL50: To define the behavior of constraint validation in the face of the `cite36†input ` element's `cite42†multiple ` attribute, `cite36†input ` elements can also have separately defined values.\nL51: To define the behavior of the `cite43†maxlength ` and `cite44†minlength ` attributes, as well as other APIs specific to the `cite40†textarea ` element, all form control with a cite37†value also have an algorithm for obtaining an API value. By default this algorithm is to simply return the control's cite37†value .\nL52: \nL53: The `cite41†select ` element does not have a cite37†value ; the cite45†selectedness of its `cite46†option ` elements is what is used instead.\nL54: ##### 4.10.18.2 Mutability\nL55: \nL56: A form control can be designated as mutable.\nL57: \nL58: This determines (by means of definitions and requirements in this specification that rely on whether an element is so designated) whether or not the user can modify the cite37†value or cite47†checkedness of a form control, or whether or not a control can be automatically prefilled.\nL59: ##### 4.10.18.3 Association of controls and forms\nL60: \nL61: A cite48†form-associated element can have a relationship with a `cite49†form ` element, which is called the element's form owner. If a cite48†form-associated element is not associated with a `cite49†form ` element, its cite50†form owner is said to be null.\nL62: \nL63: A cite48†form-associated element has an associated parser inserted flag.\nL64: \nL65: ✔MDN\nL66: \nL67: cite51†Element/input#form†developer.mozilla.org L68: \nL69: Support in all current engines.\nL70: \nL71: Firefox 1+Safari 1+Chrome 1+\nL72: \nL73: * * *\nL74: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL75: \nL76: * * *\nL77: \nL78: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 5.5+\nL79: \nL80: * * *\nL81: \nL82: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL83: \nL84: cite52†Attributes#attr-form†developer.mozilla.org L85: \nL86: Support in all current engines.\nL87: \nL88: Firefox 1+Safari≤4+Chrome 1+\nL89: \nL90: * * *\nL91: \nL92: Opera≤12.1+Edge 79+\nL93: \nL94: * * *\nL95: \nL96: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer≤6+\nL97: \nL98: * * *\nL99: \nL100: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android≤12.1+\nL101: A cite48†form-associated element is, by default, associated with its nearest ancestor `cite49†form ` element (as described below), but, if it is cite53†listed , may have a `form` attribute specified to override this.\nL102: \nL103: This feature allows authors to work around the lack of support for nested `cite49†form ` elements.\nL104: If a cite53†listed cite48†form-associated element has a `cite54†form ` attribute specified, then that attribute's value must be the cite55†ID†dom.spec.whatwg.org of a `cite49†form ` element in the element's cite56†tree†dom.spec.whatwg.org .\nL105: The rules in this section are complicated by the fact that although conforming documents or cite56†trees†dom.spec.whatwg.org will never contain nested `cite49†form ` elements, it is quite possible (e.g., using a script that performs DOM manipulation) to generate cite56†trees†dom.spec.whatwg.org that have such nested elements.\nL106: They are also complicated by rules in the HTML parser that, for historical reasons, can result in a cite48†form-associated element being associated with a `cite49†form ` element that is not its ancestor.\nL107: When a cite48†form-associated element is created, its cite50†form owner must be initialized to null (no owner).\nL108: \nL109: When a cite48†form-associated element is to be associated with a form, its cite50†form owner must be set to that form.\nL110: \nL111: When a cite53†listed cite48†form-associated element 's `cite54†form ` attribute is set, changed, or removed, then the user agent must cite57†reset the form owner of that element.\nL112: When a cite53†listed cite48†form-associated element has a `cite54†form ` attribute and the cite55†ID†dom.spec.whatwg.org of any of the elements in the cite56†tree†dom.spec.whatwg.org changes, then the user agent must cite57†reset the form owner of that cite48†form-associated element .\nL113: When a cite53†listed cite48†form-associated element has a `cite54†form ` attribute and an element with an cite55†ID†dom.spec.whatwg.org is cite58†inserted into or cite59†removed from the `cite60†Document `, or its cite61†HTML element moving steps are run, then the user agent must cite57†reset the form owner of that cite48†form-associated element .\nL114: \nL115: The form owner is also reset by the cite62†HTML element insertion steps , cite63†HTML element removing steps , and cite61†HTML element moving steps .\nL116: To reset the form owner of a cite48†form-associated element `element`:\nL117: \nL118:   1. Unset `element`'s cite64†parser inserted flag .\nL119: \nL120:   2. If all of the following are true:\nL121: \nL122:      * `element`'s cite50†form owner is not null;\nL123: \nL124:      * `element` is not cite53†listed or its `cite54†form ` content attribute is not present; and\nL125: \nL126:      * `element`'s cite50†form owner is its nearest `cite49†form ` element ancestor after the change to the ancestor chain,\nL127: \nL128: then return.\nL129: \nL130:   3. Set `element`'s cite50†form owner to null.\nL131:   4. If `element` is cite53†listed , has a `cite54†form ` content attribute, and is cite65†connected†dom.spec.whatwg.org :\nL132: \nL133:     1. If the first element in `element`'s cite56†tree†dom.spec.whatwg.org , in cite66†tree order†dom.spec.whatwg.org , to have an cite55†ID†dom.spec.whatwg.org that is cite67†identical to†infra.spec.whatwg.org `element`'s `cite54†form ` content attribute's value, is a `cite49†form ` element, then cite68†associate the `element` with that `cite49†form ` element.\nL134:   5. Otherwise, if `element` has an ancestor `cite49†form ` element, then cite68†associate `element` with the nearest such ancestor `cite49†form ` element.\nL135: \nL136: In the following non-conforming snippet\nL137: \nL138:     `...\nL139:      <form id=\"a\">\nL140:       <div id=\"b\"></div>\nL141:      </form>\nL142:      <script>\nL143:       document.getElementById('b').innerHTML =\nL144:          '<table><tr><td></form><form id=\"c\"><input id=\"d\"></table>' +\nL145:          '<input id=\"e\">';\nL146:      </script>\nL147:     ...`\nL148: the cite50†form owner of \"d\" would be the inner nested form \"c\", while the cite50†form owner of \"e\" would be the outer form \"a\".\nL149: \nL150: This happens as follows: First, the \"e\" node gets associated with \"c\" in the cite69†HTML parser . Then, the `cite70†innerHTML ` algorithm moves the nodes from the temporary document to the \"b\" element. At this point, the nodes see their ancestor chain change, and thus all the \"magic\" associations done by the parser are reset to normal ancestor associations.\nL151: This example is a non-conforming document, though, as it is a violation of the content models to nest `cite49†form ` elements, and there is a cite71†parse error for the `</form>` tag.\nL152: \nL153: ``element`.cite72†form `\nL154: \nL155: ✔MDN\nL156: \nL157: cite73†HTMLObjectElement/form†developer.mozilla.org L158: \nL159: Support in all current engines.\nL160: \nL161: Firefox 1+Safari 3+Chrome 1+\nL162: \nL163: * * *\nL164: \nL165: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL166: \nL167: * * *\nL168: \nL169: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 5.5+\nL170: \nL171: * * *\nL172: \nL173: Firefox Android?Safari iOS 1+Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL174: cite74†HTMLSelectElement/form†developer.mozilla.org L175: \nL176: Support in all current engines.\nL177: \nL178: Firefox 1+Safari 3+Chrome 1+\nL179: \nL180: * * *\nL181: \nL182: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL183: \nL184: * * *\nL185: \nL186: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 5.5+\nL187: \nL188: * * *\nL189: \nL190: Firefox Android?Safari iOS 1+Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL191: \nL192: \nL193: \nL194: Returns the element's cite50†form owner .\nL195: \nL196: Returns null if there isn't one.\nL197: cite53†Listed cite48†form-associated elements except for cite75†form-associated custom elements have a `form` IDL attribute, which, on getting, must return the element's cite50†form owner , or null if there isn't one.\nL198: \nL199: ✔MDN\nL200: \nL201: cite76†ElementInternals/form†developer.mozilla.org L202: \nL203: Support in all current engines.\nL204: \nL205: Firefox 98+Safari 16.4+Chrome 77+\nL206: \nL207: * * *\nL208: \nL209: Opera?Edge 79+\nL210: \nL211: * * *\nL212: \nL213: Edge (Legacy)?Internet Explorer No\nL214: \nL215: * * *\nL216: \nL217: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android?\nL218: cite75†Form-associated custom elements don't have a `cite72†form ` IDL attribute. Instead, their `cite77†ElementInternals ` object has a `form` IDL attribute. On getting, it must throw a cite78†\"`NotSupportedError`\"†webidl.spec.whatwg.org `cite79†DOMException†webidl.spec.whatwg.org ` if the cite80†target element is not a cite75†form-associated custom element . Otherwise, it must return the element's cite50†form owner , or null if there isn't one.\nL219: #### 4.10.19 Attributes common to form controls\nL220: ##### 4.10.19.1 Naming form controls: the `cite81†name ` attribute\nL221: \nL222: ✔MDN\nL223: \nL224: cite82†Element/input#name†developer.mozilla.org L225: \nL226: Support in all current engines.\nL227: \nL228: Firefox 1+Safari 1+Chrome 1+\nL229: \nL230: * * *\nL231: \nL232: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL233: \nL234: * * *\nL235: \nL236: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 5.5+\nL237: \nL238: * * *\nL239: \nL240: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL241: The `name` content attribute gives the name of the form control, as used in cite23†form submission and in the `cite49†form ` element's `cite83†elements ` object. If the attribute is specified, its value must not be the empty string or `isindex`.\nL242: A number of user agents historically implemented special support for first-in-form text controls with the name `isindex`, and this specification previously defined related user agent requirements for it. However, some user agents subsequently dropped that special support, and the related requirements were removed from this specification. So, to avoid problematic reinterpretations in legacy user agents, the name `isindex` is no longer allowed.\nL243: Other than `isindex`, any non-empty value for `cite84†name ` is allowed. An cite85†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org match for the name `_charset_` is special: if used as the name of a cite86†Hidden control with no `cite87†value ` attribute, then during submission the `cite87†value ` attribute is automatically given a value consisting of the submission character encoding.\nL244: DOM clobbering is a common cause of security issues. Avoid using the names of built-in form properties with the `cite81†name ` content attribute.\nL245: \nL246: In this example, the `cite36†input ` element overrides the built-in `cite88†method ` property:\nL247: \nL248:     `let form = document.createElement(\"form\");\nL249:     let input = document.createElement(\"input\");\nL250:     form.appendChild(input);\nL251: \nL252:     form.method;           // => \"get\"\nL253:     input.name = \"method\"; // DOM clobbering occurs here\nL254:     form.method === input; // => true`\nL255: Since the input name takes precedence over built-in form properties, the JavaScript reference `form.method` will point to the `cite36†input ` element named \"method\" instead of the built-in `cite88†method ` property.\nL256: ##### 4.10.19.2 Submitting element directionality: the `cite89†dirname ` attribute\nL257: \nL258: ✔MDN\nL259: \nL260: cite90†Element/input#dirname†developer.mozilla.org L261: \nL262: Support in all current engines.\nL263: \nL264: Firefox 116+Safari 6+Chrome 17+\nL265: \nL266: * * *\nL267: \nL268: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL269: \nL270: * * *\nL271: \nL272: Edge (Legacy)?Internet Explorer No\nL273: \nL274: * * *\nL275: \nL276: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL277: The `dirname` attribute on a form control element enables the submission of cite91†the directionality of the element, and gives the name of the control that contains this value during cite23†form submission . If such an attribute is specified, its value must not be the empty string.\nL278: In this example, a form contains a text control and a submission button:\nL279: \nL280:     `<form action=\"addcomment.cgi\" method=post>\nL281:      <p><label>Comment: <input type=text name=\"comment\" dirname=\"comment.dir\" required></label></p>\nL282:      <p><button name=\"mode\" type=submit value=\"add\">Post Comment</button></p>\nL283:     </form>`\nL284: When the user submits the form, the user agent includes three fields, one called \"comment\", one called \"comment.dir\", and one called \"mode\"; so if the user types \"Hello\", the submission body might be something like:\nL285: \nL286:     comment=Hello&comment.dir=ltr&mode=add\nL287: \nL288: If the user manually switches to a right-to-left writing direction and enters \"مرحبا\", the submission body might be something like:\nL289: \nL290:     comment=%D9%85%D8%B1%D8%AD%D8%A8%D8%A7&comment.dir=rtl&mode=add\nL291: ##### 4.10.19.3 Limiting user input length: the `cite43†maxlength ` attribute\nL292: \nL293: A form control `maxlength` attribute, controlled by the cite92†dirty value flag , declares a limit on the number of characters a user can input. The number of characters is measured using cite93†length†infra.spec.whatwg.org and, in the case of `cite40†textarea ` elements, with all newlines normalized to a single character (as opposed to CRLF pairs).\nL294: If an element has its cite43†form control `maxlength` attribute specified, the attribute's value must be a cite94†valid non-negative integer . If the attribute is specified and applying the cite95†rules for parsing non-negative integers to its value results in a number, then that number is the element's maximum allowed value length. If the attribute is omitted or parsing its value results in an error, then there is no cite96†maximum allowed value length .\nL295: Constraint validation: If an element has a cite96†maximum allowed value length , its cite92†dirty value flag is true, its cite37†value was last changed by a user edit (as opposed to a change made by a script), and the cite93†length†infra.spec.whatwg.org of the element's cite97†API value is greater than the element's cite96†maximum allowed value length , then the element is cite98†suffering from being too long .\nL296: User agents may prevent the user from causing the element's cite97†API value to be set to a value whose cite93†length†infra.spec.whatwg.org is greater than the element's cite96†maximum allowed value length .\nL297: \nL298: In the case of `cite40†textarea ` elements, the cite97†API value and cite37†value differ. In particular, cite99†newline normalization†infra.spec.whatwg.org is applied before the cite96†maximum allowed value length is checked (whereas the cite100†textarea wrapping transformation is not applied).\nL299: ##### 4.10.19.4 Setting minimum input length requirements: the `cite44†minlength ` attribute\nL300: \nL301: A form control `minlength` attribute, controlled by the cite92†dirty value flag , declares a lower bound on the number of characters a user can input. The \"number of characters\" is measured using cite93†length†infra.spec.whatwg.org and, in the case of `cite40†textarea ` elements, with all newlines normalized to a single character (as opposed to CRLF pairs).\nL302: The `cite44†minlength ` attribute does not imply the `required` attribute. If the form control has no `required` attribute, then the value can still be omitted; the `cite44†minlength ` attribute only kicks in once the user has entered a value at all. If the empty string is not allowed, then the `required` attribute also needs to be set.\nL303: If an element has its cite44†form control `minlength` attribute specified, the attribute's value must be a cite94†valid non-negative integer . If the attribute is specified and applying the cite95†rules for parsing non-negative integers to its value results in a number, then that number is the element's minimum allowed value length. If the attribute is omitted or parsing its value results in an error, then there is no cite101†minimum allowed value length .\nL304: If an element has both a cite96†maximum allowed value length and a cite101†minimum allowed value length , the cite101†minimum allowed value length must be smaller than or equal to the cite96†maximum allowed value length .\nL305: Constraint validation: If an element has a cite101†minimum allowed value length , its cite92†dirty value flag is true, its cite37†value was last changed by a user edit (as opposed to a change made by a script), its cite37†value is not the empty string, and the cite93†length†infra.spec.whatwg.org of the element's cite97†API value is less than the element's cite101†minimum allowed value length , then the element is cite102†suffering from being too short .\nL306: In this example, there are four text controls. The first is required, and has to be at least 5 characters long. The other three are optional, but if the user fills one in, the user has to enter at least 10 characters.\nL307: \nL308:     `<form action=\"/events/menu.cgi\" method=\"post\">\nL309:      <p><label>Name of Event: <input required minlength=5 maxlength=50 name=event></label></p>\nL310:      <p><label>Describe what you would like for breakfast, if anything:\nL311:         <textarea name=\"breakfast\" minlength=\"10\"></textarea></label></p>\nL312:      <p><label>Describe what you would like for lunch, if anything:\nL313:         <textarea name=\"lunch\" minlength=\"10\"></textarea></label></p>\nL314:      <p><label>Describe what you would like for dinner, if anything:\nL315:         <textarea name=\"dinner\" minlength=\"10\"></textarea></label></p>\nL316:      <p><input type=submit value=\"Submit Request\"></p>\nL317:     </form>`\nL318: ##### 4.10.19.5 Enabling and disabling form controls: the `cite103†disabled ` attribute\nL319: \nL320: ✔MDN\nL321: \nL322: cite104†Attributes/disabled†developer.mozilla.org L323: \nL324: Support in all current engines.\nL325: \nL326: Firefox 1+Safari 4+Chrome 1+\nL327: \nL328: * * *\nL329: \nL330: Opera?Edge 79+\nL331: \nL332: * * *\nL333: \nL334: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer Yes\nL335: \nL336: * * *\nL337: \nL338: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android?\nL339: \nL340: cite104†Attributes/disabled†developer.mozilla.org L341: \nL342: Support in all current engines.\nL343: \nL344: Firefox 4+Safari 6+Chrome 20+\nL345: \nL346: * * *\nL347: \nL348: Opera 12+Edge 79+\nL349: * * *\nL350: \nL351: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer Yes\nL352: \nL353: * * *\nL354: \nL355: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12+\nL356: \nL357: cite104†Attributes/disabled†developer.mozilla.org L358: \nL359: Support in all current engines.\nL360: \nL361: Firefox 1+Safari 1+Chrome 1+\nL362: \nL363: * * *\nL364: \nL365: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL366: \nL367: * * *\nL368: \nL369: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 5.5+\nL370: \nL371: * * *\nL372: \nL373: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL374: \nL375: cite104†Attributes/disabled†developer.mozilla.org L376: Support in all current engines.\nL377: \nL378: Firefox 1+Safari 3+Chrome 1+\nL379: \nL380: * * *\nL381: \nL382: Opera?Edge 79+\nL383: \nL384: * * *\nL385: \nL386: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer Yes\nL387: \nL388: * * *\nL389: \nL390: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android?\nL391: \nL392: cite104†Attributes/disabled†developer.mozilla.org L393: \nL394: Support in all current engines.\nL395: \nL396: Firefox 1+Safari 4+Chrome 1+\nL397: \nL398: * * *\nL399: \nL400: Opera 12.1+Edge 79+\nL401: \nL402: * * *\nL403: \nL404: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 6+\nL405: \nL406: * * *\nL407: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android?Samsung Internet?Opera Android 12.1+\nL408: \nL409: The `disabled` content attribute is a cite105†boolean attribute .\nL410: \nL411: The `cite106†disabled ` attribute for `cite46†option ` elements and the `cite107†disabled ` attribute for `cite108†optgroup ` elements are defined separately.\nL412: \nL413: A form control is disabled if any of the following are true:\nL414:   * the element is a `cite109†button `, `cite36†input `, `cite41†select `, `cite40†textarea `, or cite75†form-associated custom element , and the `cite103†disabled ` attribute is specified on this element (regardless of its value); or\nL415: \nL416:   * the element is a descendant of a `cite110†fieldset ` element whose `cite111†disabled ` attribute is specified, and the element is not a descendant of that `cite110†fieldset ` element's first `cite112†legend ` element child, if any.\nL417: A form control that is cite113†disabled must prevent any `cite114†click†w3c.github.io ` events that are cite115†queued on the cite116†user interaction task source from being dispatched on the element.\nL418: Being cite113†disabled does not prevent all modifications to the form control. For example, the control's cite37†value or cite47†checkedness could be modified programmatically from JavaScript. Or, they could be indirectly modified by user action, e.g., if other non-disabled elements in the control's cite117†radio button group were modified.\nL419: \nL420: Constraint validation: If an element is cite113†disabled , it is cite118†barred from constraint validation .\nL421: ##### 4.10.19.6 Form submission attributes\nL422: \nL423: ✔MDN\nL424: \nL425: cite119†Element/form#Attributes_for_form_submission†developer.mozilla.org L426: \nL427: Support in all current engines.\nL428: \nL429: Firefox 4+Safari 10.1+Chrome 10+\nL430: \nL431: * * *\nL432: \nL433: Opera?Edge 79+\nL434: \nL435: * * *\nL436: \nL437: Edge (Legacy)12+Internet Explorer 10+\nL438: \nL439: * * *\nL440: \nL441: Firefox Android?Safari iOS?Chrome Android?WebView Android 37+Samsung Internet?Opera Android?\nL442: Attributes for form submission can be specified both on `cite49†form ` elements and on cite120†submit buttons (elements that represent buttons that submit forms, e.g. an `cite36†input ` element whose `cite121†type ` attribute is in the cite122†Submit Button state).\nL443: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nForm Instructions | Web Accessibility Initiative (WAI) | W3C (https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/instructions/)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/instructions/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 240\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDisclosure (Show/Hide) Pattern | APG | WAI | W3C (https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/disclosure/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 69\n"
